# Line endings.
#
# Every text file in the distribution is stored and checked out with
# Windows line endings, CRLF.  The package is authored on Windows, and a
# user who takes a zip from the repository host, or a raw file, gets the
# bytes that are committed -- git's own normalization would hand that user
# LF, which is not what the rest of the package looks like.  So the files
# are committed verbatim:
#
#     * -text
#
# means "never translate", in either direction, whatever core.autocrlf is
# set to locally.  It is also the right rule for the binaries in CODE/ --
# the executables and the MinGW DLLs -- which must never be touched.
#
# The cost of verbatim storage is that git no longer silently fixes a file
# saved with LF by an editor on Linux or macOS.  Save as CRLF there, or run
#
#     unix2dos <file>            (or: sed -i 's/$/\r/' on a file with LF)
#
# before committing.  `git ls-files --eol` shows what is actually stored.
#
# CRLF is safe everywhere it is used here.  gfortran strips a trailing CR
# both when compiling a fixed-form source and when reading a formatted
# record at run time, so the Fortran sources and every input deck are
# unaffected on Linux and macOS.  Python's universal-newline mode does the
# same for the tools in CODE/, which are imported or run as `python3
# CODE/tool.py` and never executed through their shebang -- install.sh
# places them 644 for exactly that reason.  toolbox/files.py strips CR
# itself, and keeps a deck's own terminator when it rewrites it.

* -text

# The shell scripts are the one exception, and it is a hard one: a script
# checked out with CRLF fails on Linux with
#
#     /bin/bash^M: bad interpreter: No such file or directory
#
# which says nothing about the real cause.  They are pinned to LF, on
# every platform.

*.sh text eol=lf
CODE/unix/* text eol=lf

# The wrappers in CODE/unix/ carry no extension -- they are typed as
# `rcg`, `rcn2`, `add_eav` and so on -- so the directory rule above is
# what covers them, and it has to stay ahead of any rule that would
# treat an extensionless file as binary.
#
# Windows batch files need no rule of their own any more: cmd.exe wants
# CRLF -- a label or a goto at the end of a line without CR has been known
# to misbehave -- and `* -text` on a CRLF working copy is what they get.
